From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] PM / devfreq: add the Allwinner A733 DSU devfreq driver

What. A devfreq driver for the A733's DynamIQ Shared Unit (L3 cache and
snoop filter of all eight cores), with a governor of its own fed by a
cpufreq transition notifier. It carries the policy of Allwinner's BSP
driver: the DSU runs at 75 % of the fastest core, rounded down to an
operating point, but never above the highest point whose voltage the A55
cluster already holds on the rail the two share; while the A76 runs at
1488 MHz or more, the DSU stays at least at the highest point of its
lowest voltage. The OPP table is the vendor's per-bin voltage table
(arm64-dts-a733-let-the-dsu-follow-the-cpu-policies); the driver selects
the column by speed bin like the CPU tables do: opp-supported-hw bit
<bin>, prop_name "speed<bin>", index 0 = the unnamed worst case.

Why. UM V1.00 5.1.4.1 (page 244) recommends running the cluster at no
less than 75% of the frequency of the fastest core. Boot0
leaves the DSU at 780 MHz, and the DSU clock limits L3-sized and DRAM
latency.

Ported from: Allwinner BSP drivers/dsufreq/sunxi-dsufreq.c, Copyright (c)
2020-2023 Allwinner Technology Co., Ltd., author panzhijian
<panzhijian@allwinnertech.com>, radxa/allwinner-bsp aiot-linux-v1.4.9
(194b5eb3), unchanged in cubie-aiot-v1.5.0 (c83016d2). Read in full; the
policy is re-derived above. Structure after mtk-cci-devfreq.c (OPP core,
devm) and tegra30-devfreq.c (a governor registered by its driver).

Where the vendor driver differs, and what this one does:
  - Ordering by hooks in its own cpufreq-dt (set_dsufreq_cb, a scale-down
    call before and a scale-up call after the CPU OPP change); mainline
    has no such hooks. Here the DSU holds its own consumer on the shared
    regulator, so the regulator core keeps the rail at the DSU's request
    until the DSU has come down -- ordering-safe without touching
    cpufreq-dt.
  - It binds a "dsu" OPP regulator (the board DT sets dsu-supply) but only
    ever sets the clock, so no voltage is voted and nothing holds the rail
    while the A55 lowers it. Here the OPP core sets voltage and clock in
    the right order.
  - The voltage cap compares for equality: an A55 voltage between two
    DSU voltages drops the DSU to its lowest cap. Here: the highest point
    not above the A55's voltage.
  - Which policy is the A55 is assumed from CPU order (policy[0]). Here:
    the policy holding the CPU whose cpu-supply is our dsu-supply, read
    from DT per policy (cpu-supply sits on one CPU per cluster). An offline
    CPU is skipped instead of deferring the probe forever; an offline A76
    cluster leaves the DSU following the A55 alone.
  - A global singleton and a static init flag; BUG_ON() when the lowest
    DSU voltage exceeds the lowest A55 voltage; of_iomap() checked with
    IS_ERR(). Here: per-device state, probe refused with a message.
  - A "dsu_cooling" knob writes bits 12:4 of CLUSTER_CFG + 0x10
    (0x08860010), an offset the UM's CLUSTER_CFG register list does not
    have (UM V1.00 5.1.5.4, page 250: 0x0004 is followed by 0x1000). Not
    ported.
  - Its own sysfs class; here the standard devfreq interface
    (available_frequencies, trans_stat, min/max_freq).
  - The target is snapped to an exact operating point here, because
    devfreq's default rounding is upwards and would cross the cap.

Speed bin: judged like sun60i_a733_efuse_xlate() in sun50i-cpufreq-nvmem.c
-- a programmed second copy (bits 23:16), bin 0, a bin beyond 31, or a bin
not described by every OPP table selects index 0, the worst-case column.
"Every table" is the DSU's own ANDed with each CPU table, the rule
sunxi_cpufreq_dt_speed_mask() applies across the CPUs, so the DSU never
runs a column for a bin the CPU side has rejected: with today's CPU tables
(bins 4 and 5) the DSU's other columns are carried but not selected. One
difference: an unreadable speed cell fails the CPU driver's probe, and
selects the worst case here (the DSU then waits for cpufreq anyway).

Speed-bin fallback: the CPU side now falls back to its index 1
(the vendor's vf0100 set), the DSU keeps index 0. Both read the unnamed
worst-case voltages, and the DSU stays capped by the A55's vote.

Rail: that the DSU shares DCDC5 with the A55 was a vendor-DT hint (no DSU
net in the datasheet, the board or the reference schematic). An edge
experiment on the 6 GiB board (DSU moved by hand
with AXI/GIC/APB at DSU/4, both clusters pinned) settles it: at DCDC5
800 mV the DSU passes 1378 MHz and fails 1430 MHz; at 840 mV it passes
1482 MHz and fails 1534 MHz; at 800 mV with DCDC3 (A76) raised to 900 mV,
1482 MHz fails within 0.3 s. Every failure is an undefined-instruction oops.

Identity: at probe the driver reads CLUSTERIDR_EL1, CLUSTERCFR_EL1 (DSU TRM
100453 r4p1, B1.7/B1.9: no traps or enables from EL1/EL2) and the L3's
CCSIDR_EL1, and prints them raw and decoded, so the 1 MiB L3 (UM V1.00
page 23) and its geometry, which the UM gives only as the IP's option list
(page 261, "optional 16-way"), are read off the silicon on every boot.

A min_freq written to the devfreq device overrides the policy, cap
included; the DSU's own vote then raises the shared rail. A transition
notifier rules out cpufreq fast switching; cpufreq-dt does not implement it.

AXI: with the optional "axi" clock (clk-sunxi-ng-a733-dsu-axi-divider)
the AXI runs at DSU/1 up to 637 MHz and at DSU/2 above -- 637 MHz being the
highest AXI rate run on silicon (DSU 1274 MHz at /2). Going up, the
divider moves to /2 before the DSU; going down, the DSU moves first, so no
step, the PERI0_600M park included, crosses 637 MHz. UM 5.1.4.1 allows 1:1
or 2:1; the vendor keeps 2:1 everywhere. GIC and APB stay at boot0's /2
(they cannot divide by 1).

Verified: fresh 6.18.54 tree 250/250 at fuzz 0; compile-check green with no
warning at sun60i-a733-dsu.o; check-defconfig clean. On silicon, 6 GiB
board (bin 4), 6.18.54 test kernel, 2026-09-29:
  - probe: "speed bin 4", "9 points 312-1274 MHz, floor 910 MHz";
  - policy proof, both policies pinned through eleven A55/A76
    pairs: the DSU PLL (0x08873000, EXT_DIVP from 0x0887301c) lands on the
    point the policy names in 11/11 cases, and DCDC5 reads exactly the A55's
    voltage in every one -- the DSU never raises the rail; 312 MHz is
    26 x 48 / 4, 598 MHz is 26 x 46 / 2; A76 at 2002 MHz with idle A55
    gives the 910 MHz floor at 800 mV;
  - 30 min at defaults (schedutil, nothing pinned), stress-ng --verify in
    all/A55/A76/idle phases: 45 phases failed 0, 26 174 DSU changes, 953 s
    at 1274 MHz (AXI/GIC/APB 637 MHz), max 71.7 C, no DSU kernel line.

AXI verified: 6 GiB board (bin 4), 6.18.54-t896, 2026-09-29:
policy proof 11/11 with AXI /1 at 312 and 598 MHz and /2 above; 30 min at
defaults 45 phases failed 0, 10 262 DSU changes (about 2 300 straight
between 312 and 1274 MHz, across the 637 MHz limit), AXI /1 in exactly the
samples at 312/598 MHz, max 71.6 C, no DSU kernel line. Bin 5
(8 GiB board): policy proof 11/11 and 45 soak phases failed 0 on the
policy without the AXI divider.

Assisted-by: Claude
---

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/interconnect/allwinner,sun60i-a733-dsu.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/interconnect/allwinner,sun60i-a733-dsu.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..628fd05
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/interconnect/allwinner,sun60i-a733-dsu.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/interconnect/allwinner,sun60i-a733-dsu.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Allwinner A733 DynamIQ Shared Unit frequency scaling
+
+maintainers:
+  - DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
+
+description:
+  The DynamIQ Shared Unit (DSU) of the Allwinner A733 holds the L3 cache and
+  the snoop filter shared by its Cortex-A55 and Cortex-A76 cores. Its clock
+  can follow the CPU frequency policies; its voltage comes from the supply it
+  shares with the Cortex-A55 cluster. The OPP table carries one voltage per
+  CPU speed bin (opp-microvolt-speed<bin>, <bin> the raw fuse value) and an
+  unnamed worst-case voltage for parts without a usable bin.
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: allwinner,sun60i-a733-dsu
+
+  clocks:
+    minItems: 1
+    items:
+      - description: The DSU clock multiplexer of the CPU clock controller.
+      - description:
+          The DSU's AXI clock divider. With it, the driver runs the AXI at
+          DSU/1 at the low operating points and at DSU/2 above them.
+
+  clock-names:
+    minItems: 1
+    items:
+      - const: dsu
+      - const: axi
+
+  operating-points-v2: true
+
+  opp-table:
+    type: object
+
+  dsu-supply:
+    description:
+      The regulator that supplies the DSU, shared with the Cortex-A55
+      cluster.
+
+  nvmem-cells:
+    items:
+      - description: The eFuse word that holds the CPU speed bin in bits 15:8.
+
+  nvmem-cell-names:
+    items:
+      - const: speed
+
+dependencies:
+  nvmem-cells: [ nvmem-cell-names ]
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - clocks
+  - operating-points-v2
+  - dsu-supply
+
+additionalProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-cpu-ccu.h>
+
+    dsu {
+        compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-dsu";
+        clocks = <&cpupll_ccu CLK_CPU_DSU>, <&cpupll_ccu CLK_CPU_DSU_AXI>;
+        clock-names = "dsu", "axi";
+        operating-points-v2 = <&dsu_opp_table>;
+        dsu-supply = <&reg_dcdc5>;
+        nvmem-cells = <&cpu_speed_grade>;
+        nvmem-cell-names = "speed";
+    };
+
+    dsu_opp_table: opp-table-dsu {
+        compatible = "operating-points-v2";
+
+        opp-780000000 {
+            opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <780000000>;
+            opp-microvolt = <800000 800000 1050000>;
+            opp-microvolt-speed4 = <800000 800000 1050000>;
+            opp-microvolt-speed5 = <800000 800000 1050000>;
+            opp-supported-hw = <0x00000031>;
+        };
+
+        opp-1066000000 {
+            opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1066000000>;
+            opp-microvolt = <950000 950000 1050000>;
+            opp-microvolt-speed4 = <840000 840000 1050000>;
+            opp-microvolt-speed5 = <800000 800000 1050000>;
+            opp-supported-hw = <0x00000031>;
+        };
+    };
+...
